.TH auto-su+j-toggle 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-su+j-toggle - Enable or disable the UFS2 journal

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-su+j-toggle new-state filesystem
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
new-state   on|off
filesystem  The mount point of the UFS2 filesystem, or "all"
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-su+j-toggle
command enables or disables UFS2 soft updates journalling for one
or more UFS2 filesystems.

Two reboots are typically required to affect the change
since it can only take effect while the filesystem is mounted
in read-only mode.  The
.B auto-su+j-toggle
command takes care of this automatically, by scheduling the change
and a second reboot to happen while still in single-user mode
during the next reboot.

Disabling the journal is recommended on systems with no battery
backup for the controller's write cache, and where losing ~30
seconds worth of newly written data is acceptable.  This is
typically the case for a desktop or laptop system, but not for
a busy file server.  See the explanation below.

Enabling a journal typically results is fewer lost files in
the event of a power outage or other improper shutdown.  However,
on disks with a write-caching controller (i.e. most of them)
and no battery backup,
journals cannot be trusted, since data in the cache will be lost
if power is lost before the controller copies the cache to the media.
The operating system cannot control or detect this situation, so it
will not run fsck(8) in every case where it is needed.  Hence, the
filesystem may be left in an inconsistent state after rebooting.
This can lead to kernel panics in rare cases.

Disabling the journal risks losing more files in the event of an
improper shutdown, since data written to a filesystem are only
buffered in memory and therefore not committed to non-volatile
media as frequently.  However, buffers are synced to the disk
frequently (typically about every 30 seconds), so the benefit of a
journal is only meaningful where losing 30 seconds worth of newly
written data is a major problem.

Disabling the journal also results in better write throughput,
since data can accumulate in RAM buffers, and then be written
efficiently as a block, and only once.  With a journal, data are
written to disk twice: First to the journal, and then moved from
the journal to the files.  Since RAM is many thousands of times
faster than storage media (even SSDs), this can have a very
noticeable impact on write performance.  It also reduces wear on
the storage media, which is especially important for SSDs, where
each cell has a limited number of write cycles before it wears out.

Lastly, disabling the journal ensures filesystem integrity after
a power loss when the disk controller's write cache has no battery
backup.  The operating system is more likely to know that data were
lost, and will trigger an fsck(8) during the boot process to restore
consistency.

On some filesystems, such as Linux's EXT, fsck(8)
has been known to take hours in some cases, stronly motivating the
use of a journal.  However, fsck(8) on FreeBSD's UFS2 filesystem
rarely takes more than a few minutes, even on large disks with
many files, so the impact os a journal on boot time is not
typically critical.  Waiting a few more minutes to boot the system
in the rare event of an improper shutdown is perfectly acceptable
in typical use cases.

This command is specific to FreeBSD + UFS2.
For similar control of ZFS filesystems, check the documentation
on the ZFS intent log (ZIL), which is comparable to a journal.
The ZIL can be disabled via a sysctl in /boot/loader.conf:

vfs.zfs.zil_disable="1"

Disabling the ZIL has the same drawbacks as disabling the UFS2
journal, so use with caution.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-su+j-toggle
.ad
.fi

